# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Terminal gateway configuration. Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and edit.
# The gateway loads this via `node --env-file-if-exists=.env`.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ---- Bind / network ----
# PORT=3007
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# Comma-separated browser origins allowed for CSRF/CSWSH checks.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3007
# Set to 1 when behind a trusted reverse proxy (honors X-Forwarded-For, marks
# the session cookie Secure).
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# ---- Auth (single user; see docs/DEPLOYMENT.md) ----
# GATEWAY_AUTH_USER=admin
# Production: a scrypt hash from `pnpm --filter @sparklab/terminal-gateway hash-password`.
# GATEWAY_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH=scrypt$...
# Dev/test only: plaintext password, hashed once at startup.
# GATEWAY_AUTH_PASSWORD=changeme

# ---- Web Push notifications ("your job finished") ----
# Generate a keypair ONCE with:
#   pnpm --filter @sparklab/terminal-gateway generate-vapid
# then paste the two keys below. Keep them STABLE — rotating invalidates every
# existing browser subscription. If these are absent the push feature is simply
# disabled (endpoints report not-configured, the poll loop never starts) and the
# gateway boots and behaves exactly as before. See docs/PUSH-NOTIFICATIONS-PLAN.md.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Contact URI (mailto: or https:) the push service can reach you at.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com

# ---- CLI turn-finished hook notifications (POST /api/push/hook-notify) ----
# A SEPARATE bearer token (never GATEWAY_API_TOKEN/KANBAN_API_TOKEN) that a
# Claude Code / Codex CLI hook script uses to tell the gateway "this session's
# current turn just finished" — closes the gap where the shell-transition poll
# loop above stays silent for an entire interactive claude/codex session. Scoped
# to exactly this one route. Absent => the route always 401s (feature inert).
# Generate with: pnpm --filter @sparklab/terminal-gateway generate-hook-token
# See docs/HOOK-NOTIFICATIONS-SETUP.md.
# HOOK_NOTIFY_TOKEN=

# ---- Codex CLI agent tool (POST /api/sessions/:id/codex) ----
# The agent's `run_codex` tool runs `codex exec` non-interactively in a session's
# cwd. These are optional; the defaults assume a `codex` on PATH. If the binary
# is absent the tool simply reports "not installed" (503) — nothing else changes.
# Override the invocation (JSON array or a plain path) — mainly for tests:
# CODEX_COMMAND=codex
# Per-run wall-clock timeout in ms (default 180000); a hung run returns 504.
# CODEX_TIMEOUT_MS=180000

# ---- Agentic AI Creator run engine (/api/agentic/*) ----
# The run engine spawns each agent-task as a DETACHED tmux job (prefix "agrun-",
# never "web-") on the target session's server, running codex/claude via the
# provider seam. All optional; defaults keep the gateway booting unchanged and
# the engine idle until a run is started. See docs/AGENTIC-AI-CREATOR-PLAN.md.
# Persisted run/agent/app store (mirrors AGENTIC_FILE default under data/).
# AGENTIC_FILE=./data/agentic.json
# Per-run scratch base — MUST be ABSOLUTE on the TARGET server (a relative path
# is meaningless over ssh). Local default lives under the gateway data dir; a
# remote host resolves $HOME/.cache/agentic-runs unless AGENT_RUNS_DIR is set
# (absolute) in the remote login environment.
# AGENT_RUNS_DIR=/absolute/path/to/agentic-runs
# The `claude` invocation (JSON array or a plain path), mirrors CODEX_COMMAND.
# CLAUDE_COMMAND=claude
# claude --permission-mode for the read-only tier (iter2 allows plan|acceptEdits;
# workspace-write always maps to acceptEdits).
# CLAUDE_DEFAULT_PERMISSION_MODE=plan
# Per-STEP wall-clock timeout in ms (default 1800000 = 30m); a hung step is
# killed and marked failed.
# AGENT_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000
# Reserved (no GC in iter2): how long a terminal run is retained.
# AGENT_RUN_RETENTION_MS=604800000
# Prompt / step-output byte caps (prompt over the cap is rejected at startRun;
# the log tail on GET is bounded to the output cap).
# AGENT_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES=16384
# AGENT_OUTPUT_MAX_BYTES=131072
# Max concurrent active runs (startRun returns 429 too_many_runs beyond this).
# AGENT_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=4
# Optional GLOBAL default spawn cap. When set to a positive integer, this caps
# ALL runs whose budget omits maxSpawns, including runs with no app budget.
# Unset means no default and preserves fully unbounded spawn counts.
# AGENT_DEFAULT_MAX_SPAWNS=10
# Whole-run max age in ms (default 259200000 = 3d): a run older than this and
# still active triggers a notify-only reminder — NEVER an auto-cancel.
# AGENTIC_RUN_MAX_AGE_MS=259200000
# Parallel fan-out cap: at most this many nodes spawn per wave.
# AGENT_MAX_PARALLEL_FANOUT=4
# Run-engine poll interval in ms (mirrors PUSH_POLL_INTERVAL_MS); the loop is
# gated on >=1 active run so it never runs idle.
# AGENTIC_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=4000
# Scheduled runs are unattended: every approval fails closed, and schedule
# administration is restricted to a human cookie session (never bearer auth).
# AGENT_SCHEDULES_FILE=./data/agentic-schedules.json
AGENT_SCHEDULE_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=30000
AGENT_SCHEDULE_MAX_INTERVAL=1000
# Per-MCP-tool-call approval hold window in ms (D5, iter3). The per-run proxy
# holds an "approval"-tier tool call open for up to this long while polling the
# gateway for a human approve/reject decision. Also used by the gateway's own
# reducer-side sweep (sweepStalePendingApprovals) with a +5s grace window, so a
# pending approval always resolves even if the proxy dies mid-hold. Default
# 170000 — empirically confirmed safe up to 170s for both claude -p and codex
# exec; do not raise past a tested boundary without re-verifying.
# AGENT_MCP_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_MS=170000
#
# Per-run SCOPED MCP capability tokens (iter5, AGENTIC-AI-CREATOR-PLAN §9 D-Token).
# ENABLED BY DEFAULT. When on, each agent-task with artifact connections is handed
# a freshly-minted, short-lived, capability-scoped bearer token in its mcp.json —
# NOT the all-powerful shared GATEWAY_API_TOKEN. A leaked copy of that token can
# reach ONLY the artifact prefixes the agent is connected to (/api/pm and/or
# /api/kanban) and its OWN pending-tool-call callback — never approve/reject
# (also cookie-only), never Agentic CRUD/run, never another run's callback, and it
# is revoked when the run ends. Because these self-mint, agentic MCP + approvals
# work WITHOUT any shared GATEWAY_API_TOKEN configured. Set to 0/false to fall
# back to the shared-token behavior (which then requires GATEWAY_API_TOKEN).
# Honest residual: a scoped token still permits direct REST to its connected
# artifact, so it bounds WHICH artifact, not WHICH tool — full per-tool closure
# (children never holding a gateway token) is deferred.
# AGENT_MCP_SCOPED_TOKENS=1
# TTL for a minted scoped token. Default 3600000 (1h) — long enough for a run,
# short enough to bound exposure; the token is also revoked on run completion.
# AGENT_MCP_TOKEN_TTL_MS=3600000
#
# Per-node retry policy (richer-workflows arc; AGENTIC-RICHER-WORKFLOWS-PLAN §1).
# An agent-task node may carry retryPolicy {maxAttempts, backoffMs, retryOn}: a
# ran-and-failed attempt (nonzero exit OR per-step timeout) is respawned up to
# maxAttempts total (incl. the first; 1 = no retry, the default when absent),
# waiting backoffMs between attempts. The driver owns the retry (the reducer never
# sees a transient failure) and a crash mid-retry is recovered by boot rediscovery
# without double-counting. These two caps clamp whatever a workflow requests.
# Hard ceiling on retryPolicy.maxAttempts (default 5).
# AGENT_RETRY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# Hard ceiling on retryPolicy.backoffMs (default 60000).
# AGENT_RETRY_BACKOFF_MAX_MS=60000
# Bounded iteration caps for loopPolicy maxIterations and backoffMs.
AGENT_LOOP_MAX_ITERATIONS=8
AGENT_LOOP_BACKOFF_MAX_MS=60000
